      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Drain the soaked salt cod and pat dry. Remove any remaining bones and skin. Cut into large pieces of about 8 cm. Dust lightly in flour and shake off the excess."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat half the olive oil in a Dutch oven over medium heat. Cook the onions slowly for 15 minutes until very soft and golden. Add the anchovy fillets and mash them into the onions for 2 minutes until dissolved."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Lay the floured fish pieces over the onions in a single layer. Sprinkle over the parmesan and parsley. Pour over the remaining olive oil and the milk. The liquid should come about halfway up the fish."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Bring to the gentlest possible simmer. Cover with a tight-fitting lid and cook over the very lowest heat for 4 hours. Do not stir. You may gently rotate the pot occasionally but do not disturb the fish."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "After 4 hours the fish should be very tender and the sauce creamy. Season with black pepper only. Serve over soft polenta."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Dish too salty even after soaking"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The fish was not soaked long enough or the water was not changed frequently. Soak for the full 48 hours and change the water at least 4 times."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Milk curdling during cooking"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The heat is too high. The braise must be at the barest shiver of a simmer. Use a heat diffuser if necessary."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "issue": "Fish falling apart before 4 hours"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "This is fine. The fish is supposed to become very tender. The key is not to stir it so the pieces stay intact."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Vicenza is a landlocked city but baccala is its most famous dish. Venetian merchants brought dried Norwegian fish back along trading routes centuries ago. The Vicentini adopted it completely. There is a confraternity that guards the authentic recipe. It is always served with white polenta, never yellow."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
